QB.add(53,[{"n":1,"q":"A 12 y/o child presents with multiple types of seizures, including atonic seizures and tonic-clonic seizures, along with significant developmental delays. The parents report that the child has been experiencing these seizures since early childhood. Which of the following syndromes is NOT associated with myoclonic seizures?","o":[{"t":"JANZ Syndrome"},{"t":"Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome"},{"t":"Progressive Myoclonic Epilepsies"},{"t":"None of the above"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) None of the above"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"All the listed syndromes (JANZ Syndrome(JME), Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome, and PME) are associated with myoclonic seizures. Therefore, the correct choice indicates that none of them can be excluded from having myoclonic seizure presentations."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myoclonic Seizures Myoclonus is a sudden and brief muscle contraction that can involve one part of the body or the entire body. Physiologic Myoclonus: A common form is the sudden jerking movement observed while falling asleep. Pathologic Myoclonus: Often associated with metabolic disorders, degenerative CNS diseases, or anoxic brain injury. Myoclonic seizures are considered true epileptic events due to cortical dysfunction. EEG Findings: Shows bilaterally synchronous spike-and-slow-wave discharges immediately before the movement, along with muscle artefact during myoclonus. Myoclonic seizures often coexist with other forms of generalized seizures."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myoclonus is a sudden and brief muscle contraction that can involve one part of the body or the entire body."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Physiologic Myoclonus: A common form is the sudden jerking movement observed while falling asleep."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pathologic Myoclonus: Often associated with metabolic disorders, degenerative CNS diseases, or anoxic brain injury."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myoclonic seizures are considered true epileptic events due to cortical dysfunction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"EEG Findings: Shows bilaterally synchronous spike-and-slow-wave discharges immediately before the movement, along with muscle artefact during myoclonus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myoclonic seizures often coexist with other forms of generalized seizures."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Juvenile Myoclonic Epilepsy (JME): (Option A ruled out) Myoclonus is a predominant feature of juvenile myoclonic epilepsy (JME). Begins around puberty or early adulthood. Seizures often occur soon after waking up involving the neck, shoulders, and upper arms."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myoclonus is a predominant feature of juvenile myoclonic epilepsy (JME)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Begins around puberty or early adulthood."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Seizures often occur soon after waking up involving the neck, shoulders, and upper arms."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome: (Option B ruled out) An uncommon syndrome that includes multiple seizure types. Begins in early childhood. Myoclonic seizures involve the neck, shoulders, upper arms, and often the face."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"An uncommon syndrome that includes multiple seizure types."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Begins in early childhood."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myoclonic seizures involve the neck, shoulders, upper arms, and often the face."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Progressive Myoclonic Epilepsies (PME): (Option C ruled out) Features a combination of myoclonic seizures and tonic-clonic seizures. Treatment is usually not successful for long and patients may deteriorate over time. Often develop intellectual disability, coordination, and walking problems."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Features a combination of myoclonic seizures and tonic-clonic seizures."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Treatment is usually not successful for long and patients may deteriorate over time."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Often develop intellectual disability, coordination, and walking problems."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myoclonus is a sudden and brief muscle contraction that can involve one part of the body or the entire body."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Physiologic Myoclonus: A common form is the sudden jerking movement observed while falling asleep."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pathologic Myoclonus: Often associated with metabolic disorders, degenerative CNS diseases, or anoxic brain injury."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myoclonic seizures are considered true epileptic events due to cortical dysfunction."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"EEG Findings: Shows bilaterally synchronous spike-and-slow-wave discharges immediately before the movement, along with muscle artefact during myoclonus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myoclonic seizures often coexist with other forms of generalized seizures."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myoclonus is a predominant feature of juvenile myoclonic epilepsy (JME)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Begins around puberty or early adulthood."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Seizures often occur soon after waking up involving the neck, shoulders, and upper arms."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"An uncommon syndrome that includes multiple seizure types."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Begins in early childhood."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myoclonic seizures involve the neck, shoulders, upper arms, and often the face."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Features a combination of myoclonic seizures and tonic-clonic seizures."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Treatment is usually not successful for long and patients may deteriorate over time."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Often develop intellectual disability, coordination, and walking problems."}]},{"n":2,"q":"Match each drug with its preferred use for the corresponding type of seizure. 1. Partial seizures a. Lorazepam 2. Generalized tonic-clonic seizures b. Diazepam 3. Febrile seizures c. Valproate 4. Status epilepticus d. Carbamazepine\n1. Partial seizures a. Lorazepam\n2. Generalized tonic-clonic seizures b. Diazepam\n3. Febrile seizures c. Valproate\n4. Status epilepticus d. Carbamazepine","o":[{"t":"1-d, 2-c, 3-b, 4-a"},{"t":"1-c, 2-d, 3-b, 4-a"},{"t":"1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-c"},{"t":"1-d, 2-b, 3-a, 4-c"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) 1-d, 2-c, 3-b, 4-a"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Antiepileptic Drug Choice by Seizure Type:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Type of Seizure Anti-Epileptic Drugs"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Partial or focal seizure Lamotrigine"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Oxcarbazepine Eslicarbazepine Carbamazepine Phenytoin Levetiracetam"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Generalized tonic clonic seizure Valproate"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Lamotrigine"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Absence seizure Ethosuximide"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Valproate Lamotrigine"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Febrile seizure Diazepam (rectal) (drug of choice)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Status epilepticus Lorazepam (drug of choice)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy (Janz syndrome) Drug of choice: Valproate > Lamotrigine >"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Topiramate"},{"k":"img","v":7955},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lennox Gestaut syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"img","v":7960},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Dravet's syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Valproate (drug of choice)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Infantile spasm ( also known as West syndrome or Occasional episodes: Hypsarrhythmia) Drug of choice: ACTH hormone +/- glucocorticoids"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Associated with tuberous sclerosis (lifelong seizures): Drug of choice: Vigabatrin"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pregnancy Levetiracetam (safest in pregnancy)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Lamotrigine"}]},{"n":3,"q":"A 27-year-old woman with a history of seizures and poor medication adherence presents to the ER after a 6-minute seizure followed by another, with impaired consciousness between episodes. She is unresponsive and has labored breathing. What is the preferred drug of choice for her treatment?","o":[{"t":"Valproate"},{"t":"Levetiracetam"},{"t":"Lorazepam"},{"t":"Diazepam"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Lorazepam"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The scenario describes a seizure lasting 6 minutes with impaired consciousness during the interictal period, indicating Status Epilepticus . The drug of choice for treatment in this case is IV lorazepam."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Status epilepticus refers to either continuous seizures (‡ 5 minutes) or recurrent (‡ 2), discrete seizures with impaired consciousness between episodes (interictal period)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Types Generalized convulsive status epilepticus (GCSE) GCSE is an emergency and must be treated immediately (e.g., persistent, generalized electrographic seizures, coma, and tonic-clonic movements). Nonconvulsive status epilepticus (NCSE) E.g., persistent absence seizures or focal seizures with confusion or partially impaired consciousness, and minimal motor abnormalities."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Generalized convulsive status epilepticus (GCSE) GCSE is an emergency and must be treated immediately (e.g., persistent, generalized electrographic seizures, coma, and tonic-clonic movements)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"GCSE is an emergency and must be treated immediately (e.g., persistent, generalized electrographic seizures, coma, and tonic-clonic movements)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nonconvulsive status epilepticus (NCSE) E.g., persistent absence seizures or focal seizures with confusion or partially impaired consciousness, and minimal motor abnormalities."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"E.g., persistent absence seizures or focal seizures with confusion or partially impaired consciousness, and minimal motor abnormalities."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Management:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Generalized convulsive status epilepticus (GCSE) GCSE is an emergency and must be treated immediately (e.g., persistent, generalized electrographic seizures, coma, and tonic-clonic movements)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"GCSE is an emergency and must be treated immediately (e.g., persistent, generalized electrographic seizures, coma, and tonic-clonic movements)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nonconvulsive status epilepticus (NCSE) E.g., persistent absence seizures or focal seizures with confusion or partially impaired consciousness, and minimal motor abnormalities."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"E.g., persistent absence seizures or focal seizures with confusion or partially impaired consciousness, and minimal motor abnormalities."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"GCSE is an emergency and must be treated immediately (e.g., persistent, generalized electrographic seizures, coma, and tonic-clonic movements)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"E.g., persistent absence seizures or focal seizures with confusion or partially impaired consciousness, and minimal motor abnormalities."},{"k":"img","v":7967},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Valproate (Option A) is the drug of choice for several types of epilepsy, including generalized tonic-clonic seizures (GTCS), atypical absence seizures, Dravet’s syndrome, juvenile myoclonic epilepsy, and in cases of atypical absence seizures when ethosuximide is not prescribed."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Levetiracetam (Option B) is considered the safest option and the drug of choice for treating epilepsy during pregnancy."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diazepam (Option D) is the drug of choice for febrile seizures, with the preferred route being rectal. It is used for both treatment and prevention."}]},{"n":4,"q":"A 4 y/o girl presents to the pediatric clinic with a port-wine stain on her forehead and upper eyelid. Her mother reports that she has been experiencing frequent seizures and has some difficulty in school. O/E, the child exhibits unilateral weakness of the left arm. CT imaging reveals tram-track calcifications in the brain. Which condition is most consistent with this presentation?","o":[{"t":"Neurofibromatosis Type 1"},{"t":"Sturge-Weber Syndrome"},{"t":"Tuberous Sclerosis"},{"t":"Hypoparathyroidism"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Sturge-Weber Syndrome"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The combination of a port-wine stain in the trigeminal distribution, seizures, unilateral weakness, and tram-track calcifications aligns perfectly with the clinical features of Sturge-Weber syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Sturge-Weber Syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is a neurocutaneous disorder characterized by vascular malformations affecting the skin, brain, and eyes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Port-wine stain (cutaneous manifestation) – Usually located in the trigeminal nerve distribution on the face."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cavernous hemangioma – May also affect intraoral structures like the lip, coexisting with the facial port-wine stain."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clinical features: STURGE"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"S : Seizures – Caused by cavernous angiomas in the brain, often leading to epilepsy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"T : Tram-track gyriform calcifications – Visible calcifications on brain imaging, seen in areas of abnormal blood vessel growth."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"U : Unilateral weakness – Hemiparesis (weakness on one side of the body) opposite the facial port-wine stain."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"R : Reduced intellect – Intellectual disability is frequently observed."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"G : Glaucoma – Increased intraocular pressure, which may cause buphthalmos (enlargement of the eye)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"E : Epilepsy – Seizures are common due to underlying brain malformations."},{"k":"img","v":7973},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tram track appearance on CT Head"},{"k":"img","v":7979},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Port wine stain"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Neurofibromatosis Type 1 (Option A) : This condition is characterized by café-au-lait spots, neurofibromas, and Lisch nodules, rather than port-wine stains and tram-track calcifications, making it an unlikely diagnosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tuberous Sclerosis (Option C) : This disorder typically presents with skin findings like angiofibromas, shagreen patches, and renal angiomyolipomas, not with port-wine stains or tram-track calcifications."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hypoparathyroidism (Option D) : This condition primarily affects calcium metabolism and is not associated with the neurological or cutaneous features described, making it irrelevant to this clinical scenario."}]},{"n":5,"q":"A 7 y/o boy is brought to the pediatric clinic due to developmental delays and frequent seizures. Upon examination, the following findings were observed. MRI shows subependymal giant cell astrocytomas. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Neurofibromatosis Type 1"},{"t":"Tuberous Sclerosis"},{"t":"Sturge-Weber Syndrome"},{"t":"Wunderlich Syndrome"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Tuberous Sclerosis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The presence of facial angiofibromas, shagreen patches, hypomelanotic macules, and subependymal giant cell astrocytomas aligns perfectly with the clinical features of tuberous sclerosis."},{"k":"img","v":7904},{"k":"img","v":7909},{"k":"img","v":7914},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tuberous Sclerosis (Bourneville Disease):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Genetics : Autosomal dominant disorder caused by mutations in the TSC1 gene (chromosome 9q34, encodes hamartin) or TSC2 gene (chromosome 16p13.3, encodes tuberin)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Protein Function : Hamartin and tuberin form a complex that inhibits mTOR signalling , acting as negative regulators of the cell cycle."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Incidence : Affects ~1 in 5,000–10,000 live births."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical Features : Seizures and mental retardation . Adenoma sebaceum (facial angiofibromas)-Facial rash that appears as a spread of small pink or red spots across the cheeks and nose in a butterfly distribution."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Seizures and mental retardation ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Adenoma sebaceum (facial angiofibromas)-Facial rash that appears as a spread of small pink or red spots across the cheeks and nose in a butterfly distribution."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Seizures and mental retardation ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Adenoma sebaceum (facial angiofibromas)-Facial rash that appears as a spread of small pink or red spots across the cheeks and nose in a butterfly distribution."},{"k":"img","v":7986},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Shagreen patch- Flesh coloured orange-peel connective tissue naevi of varying sizes, usually on the lower back"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Periungual fibromas -Smooth, firm, flesh-coloured lumps that emerge from the nail folds"},{"k":"img","v":7991},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypomelanotic macules"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Renal angiomyolipomas and cardiac rhabdomyomas ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neurological Findings : Increased incidence of subependymal nodules , cortical tubers , and subependymal giant cell astrocytomas (SEGAs) ."},{"k":"img","v":7997},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Black arrow showing subependymal giant cell astrocytoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Treatment : Anticonvulsants for seizure management . mTOR inhibitors (sirolimus or everolimus) are effective in reducing SEGA size and seizures ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anticonvulsants for seizure management ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"mTOR inhibitors (sirolimus or everolimus) are effective in reducing SEGA size and seizures ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Therapeutic Intervention : SEGAs do not always require treatment but respond well to mTOR inhibitors."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anticonvulsants for seizure management ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"mTOR inhibitors (sirolimus or everolimus) are effective in reducing SEGA size and seizures ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Neurofibromatosis Type 1 (Option A) : This condition is characterized by café-au-lait spots and neurofibromas, not typically by angiofibromas or shagreen patches, making it an unlikely diagnosis."},{"k":"img","v":8002},{"k":"img","v":8008},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Sturge-Weber Syndrome (Option C) : This syndrome is primarily associated with port-wine stains and leptomeningeal angiomas, along with neurological deficits; it does not typically present with the findings noted in this patient."},{"k":"img","v":7979},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Wunderlich Syndrome (Option D) involves spontaneous renal haemorrhage, often related to renal angiomyolipomas, which can also be a feature of tuberous sclerosis but isn't the primary diagnosis here."}],"qi":[7904,7909,7914]},{"n":6,"q":"A 12 y/o boy presents to the clinic with a history of behavioural changes and progressive seizures following a measles infection at age 2. The neurologist suspects subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE). Which combination of diagnostic findings would confirm the diagnosis according to SSPE diagnostic criteria?","o":[{"t":"Typical clinical presentation, elevated CSF measles antibody titers, and normal EEG"},{"t":"Brain biopsy with characteristic histopathological findings and elevated CSF globulin levels."},{"t":"Characteristic EEG findings, elevated CSF globulin levels, and elevated CSF measles antibody titers"},{"t":"Increased T2 signal on MRI, acellular CSF, and elevated serum measles-specific IgG"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Characteristic EEG findings, elevated CSF globulin levels, and elevated CSF measles antibody titers"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This option accurately reflects the diagnostic criteria for SSPE, which requires three of five specific criteria to confirm the diagnosis. The combination of characteristic EEG findings, elevated CSF globulin, and elevated CSF measles antibody levels provides strong evidence for SSPE."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Subacute Sclerosing Panencephalitis (SSPE):"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MRI : Often normal early; later stages show increased T2 signal in the white matter of the brain and brainstem."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"EEG : Initially shows nonspecific slowing, progressing to a characteristic periodic pattern with bursts of high-voltage sharp, slow waves every 3–8 seconds, followed by attenuated \" flat\" background."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CSF findings : Acellular, with normal or mildly elevated protein; significantly elevated gamma globulin (>20% of total protein); elevated antimeasles antibody levels and presence of oligoclonal antimeasles antibodies."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Brain biopsy : Measles virus can be cultured from brain tissue; viral antigen identified via immunocytochemistry or viral genome via PCR."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum : Persistent measles-specific IgG in serum and CSF."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Diagnostic Criteria: (Options A & B ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"SSPE can be diagnosed if three out of five criteria are met:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Typical clinical presentation (progressive neurologic deterioration)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"EEG showing stereotyped periodic complexes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Brain biopsy or autopsy with characteristic histopathological findings."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"CSF globulin levels greater than 20% of total CSF protein."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated CSF measles antibody titers (‡1/4 by hemagglutination inhibition or ‡1/20 by ELISA)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"No definitive therapy is available."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Isoprinosine (Inosiplex, 100 mg/kg/day) , either alone or combined with intrathecal or intraventricular interferon-a, may prolong survival and provide clinical improvement , though this is not confirmed by controlled trials."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prognosis : Antiviral therapy is ineffective, and death typically occurs within a few years."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Increased T2 signal on MRI, acellular CSF, and elevated serum measles-specific IgG (Option D) : While increased T2 signal on MRI can be seen in SSPE, acellular CSF is not characteristic of SSPE. Moreover, elevated serum measles-specific IgG does not provide the necessary diagnostic support without fulfilling the criteria related to CSF findings."}]},{"n":7,"q":"A 10 y/o boy presents to the clinic with a year-long history of cognitive decline. His parents report increased irritability, social withdrawal, and difficulty in school. He had measles at age 2. On examination, he shows signs of myoclonus and motor difficulties. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis"},{"t":"Dravet syndrome"},{"t":"Landau-Kleffner syndrome"},{"t":"Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"SSPE is a rare, progressive neurological disorder that occurs 6-8 years after a measles infection. It is characterized by cognitive decline, personality changes, irritability, myoclonus, and motor difficulties, aligning with the patient's symptoms and history of measles."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Subacute Sclerosing Panencephalitis (SSPE)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"About SSPE is a rare, severe complication of the measles"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"virus. It typically presents in early adolescence and has a progressive course with high mortality. Vaccination has reduced measles cases, but SSPE remains a concern due to measles re-emergence."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathophysiology Caused by the measles virus , which is highly"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"contagious and transmitted via aerosols. The latency period ranges from 6-8 years post-infection , with shorter periods in younger children. The virus targets lymphocytes and epithelial cells , causing significant immunosuppression. SSPE is associated with a poor cellular immune response and persistent viral presence in the CNS. Neuronal entry and infection mechanisms are not fully understood; mutations in the virus may facilitate evasion of the immune response."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Features Symptoms typically arise 6-8 years after measles"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"infection, starting with cognitive decline. Early signs include personality changes, poor school performance, and lethargy. Stage I : Personality and behavioural changes, including irritability, dementia, lethargy, social withdrawal, and speech regression. Stage II : Progressive decline in motor function characterized by myoclonus, dyskinesia, and dystonia. Stage III : Development of extrapyramidal symptoms, posturing, and spasticity. Stage IV : Akinetic mutism, autonomic failure, or transition to a vegetative state. Progressive motor function decline occurs, with myoclonus and focal paralysis. Patients may develop seizures , leading to vegetative states or akinetic mutism. Visual symptoms may precede other signs, including retinal lesions and vision loss. Atypical presentations can involve psychiatric symptoms and rapid decline, especially in younger patients."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Dravet’s syndrome (Option B) is a severe form of epilepsy that usually presents in infancy with prolonged seizures and is often associated with developmental delays. While it involves cognitive decline and seizures, the onset is typically much earlier, and there’s no prior history of measles infection that would suggest SSPE."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Landau-Kleffner syndrome (Option C) is characterized by the loss of language skills and seizures in children, typically occurring between ages 3 and 7. While it includes cognitive decline, it does not align with the patient’s history of measles or the specific symptoms of myoclonus."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy (Option D) usually presents in adolescence with myoclonic jerks and generalized tonic-clonic seizures. It doesn't typically involve a history of measles or the specific progressive cognitive decline noted in this patient."}]},{"n":8,"q":"Match the following conditions with their respective EEGs. 1) Focal seizure a) 2) Absence seizure b) 3) Hypsarrythmia c) 4) Periodic sharp wave complexes d)\n1) Focal seizure\na)\n2) Absence seizure\nb)\n3) Hypsarrythmia\nc)\n4) Periodic sharp wave complexes\nd)","o":[{"t":"1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b"},{"t":"1-b, 2-d, 3-c, 4-a"},{"t":"1-d, 2-c, 3-b, 4-a"},{"t":"1-d, 2-b, 3-c, 4-a"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) 1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"img","v":7932},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Focal seizure -EEG shows localized spikes or sharp waves in the region of the brain corresponding to the seizure onset."},{"k":"img","v":7922},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Absence seizure -EEG exhibits 3 Hz spike-and-slow wave discharges, typically generalized and symmetric."},{"k":"img","v":7939},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hypsarrythmia -EEG presents chaotic, bursts of bilateral asynchronous high-amplitude slow waves interspersed with spikes followed by generalized voltage attenuation."},{"k":"img","v":7927},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Periodic sharp wave complexes -EEG reveals generalized synchronized high-voltage sharp waves occurring at 1–2 Hz."}],"qi":[7922,7927,7932,7939]},{"n":9,"q":"A 62 y/o man presents with rapid cognitive decline, visual disturbances, and myoclonus. An EEG shows localized frontal intermittent rhythmical delta activity (FIRDA) that evolves to periodic sharp wave complexes (PSWC) after a few weeks. Based on this presentation and EEG findings, which of the following conditions is most likely associated with his symptoms?","o":[{"t":"Hepatic encephalopathy"},{"t":"Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease"},{"t":"Renal encephalopathy"},{"t":"Alzheimer’s disease"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The progression from localized frontal intermittent rhythmical delta activity (FIRDA) to PSWC is characteristic of Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (CJD), along with the patient's rapid cognitive decline and myoclonus."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Periodic sharp wave complexes (PSWC)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The first EEG finding in Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (CJD) is often localized frontal intermittent rhythmical delta activity (FIRDA)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"FIRDA evolves to typical periodic sharp wave complexes (PSWC) after a few weeks."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PSWC is characterized by generalized synchronized high-voltage sharp waves with frequencies of 1–2 Hz."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"While PSWC usually appear generalized, they may exhibit lateralized or localized distributions in the early stages of CJD."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PSWC is one of the criteria for the clinical diagnosis of CJD."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hence, Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (CJD) presents with PSWC and myoclonic jerks."},{"k":"img","v":7927},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Periodic sharp wave complexes are seen on EEG"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Triphasic waves"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Triphasic waves are abnormal EEG waveforms characterized by high-amplitude sharp waves with three distinctive phases."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"They are seen in association with multiple clinical conditions, including encephalopathy, structural brain lesions etc."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Duration: 300 to 600 milliseconds."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Phases: First phase (negative), second phase (positive, amplitude >70 microvolts), third phase (slow negative)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Frequency: Periodic, at a rate of 0.5 Hz to 2 Hz."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Distribution: Diffuse with synchronous bifrontal and central predominance, showing fronto-occipital time lag."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Causes: Encephalopathy: Hepatic, Renal and Metabolic (e.g., hypernatremia, hyponatremia, hypoglycemia) Structural Abnormalities: Cerebral abscess Thalamic gliomas Pontine stroke Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (CJD) Alzheimer's disease Other Conditions: Ictal or postictal states Serotonin syndrome Lithium toxicity Antibiotic toxicity (e.g., cefepime)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Encephalopathy: Hepatic, Renal and Metabolic (e.g., hypernatremia, hyponatremia, hypoglycemia)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Structural Abnormalities: Cerebral abscess Thalamic gliomas Pontine stroke Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (CJD) Alzheimer's disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cerebral abscess"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thalamic gliomas"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pontine stroke"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (CJD)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alzheimer's disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other Conditions: Ictal or postictal states Serotonin syndrome Lithium toxicity Antibiotic toxicity (e.g., cefepime)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ictal or postictal states"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serotonin syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lithium toxicity"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antibiotic toxicity (e.g., cefepime)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Encephalopathy: Hepatic, Renal and Metabolic (e.g., hypernatremia, hyponatremia, hypoglycemia)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Structural Abnormalities: Cerebral abscess Thalamic gliomas Pontine stroke Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (CJD) Alzheimer's disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cerebral abscess"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thalamic gliomas"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pontine stroke"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (CJD)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alzheimer's disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other Conditions: Ictal or postictal states Serotonin syndrome Lithium toxicity Antibiotic toxicity (e.g., cefepime)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ictal or postictal states"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serotonin syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lithium toxicity"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antibiotic toxicity (e.g., cefepime)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cerebral abscess"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thalamic gliomas"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pontine stroke"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (CJD)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alzheimer's disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ictal or postictal states"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serotonin syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lithium toxicity"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antibiotic toxicity (e.g., cefepime)"},{"k":"img","v":8021},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hepatic and renal encephalopathy (Options A & C) may cause altered mental status, but not the rapid cognitive decline, myoclonus, and specific EEG findings seen in CJD."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Alzheimer’s disease (Option D) typically has a much slower progression and lacks these EEG patterns and associated myoclonus."}]},{"n":10,"q":"A 2-day-old infant born at 39 weeks gestation is being evaluated for seizures, and there is a family history of seizures. What is the most common type of seizure seen in newborns?","o":[{"t":"Benign Rolandic epilepsy"},{"t":"Subtle seizure"},{"t":"Febrile seizures"},{"t":"Myoclonic seizures"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Subtle seizure"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Subtle seizures are the most common seizure type in newborns , often presenting with changes in tone or subtle movements that often go unnoticed."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These seizures mimic normal behaviours and reactions, making them easily overlooked."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Types of Movements: Ocular Movements: Includes random eye movements, sustained conjugate tonic deviation, eyelid blinking or fluttering, eye-rolling, and nystagmus. Oral-Buccal-Lingual Movements: Such as sucking, smacking, chewing, and tongue protrusions. Progressive Movements: Including rowing, swimming, pedalling, thrashing, or struggling movements. Complex Purposeless Movements: Characterized by sudden arousal, episodic limb hyperactivity, and crying."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ocular Movements: Includes random eye movements, sustained conjugate tonic deviation, eyelid blinking or fluttering, eye-rolling, and nystagmus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oral-Buccal-Lingual Movements: Such as sucking, smacking, chewing, and tongue protrusions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Progressive Movements: Including rowing, swimming, pedalling, thrashing, or struggling movements."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Complex Purposeless Movements: Characterized by sudden arousal, episodic limb hyperactivity, and crying."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ocular Movements: Includes random eye movements, sustained conjugate tonic deviation, eyelid blinking or fluttering, eye-rolling, and nystagmus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oral-Buccal-Lingual Movements: Such as sucking, smacking, chewing, and tongue protrusions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Progressive Movements: Including rowing, swimming, pedalling, thrashing, or struggling movements."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Complex Purposeless Movements: Characterized by sudden arousal, episodic limb hyperactivity, and crying."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Benign rolandic epilepsy (Option A) is the most common type of epilepsy in children. It's also known as benign epilepsy with centrotemporal spikes (BECTS)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms- Twitching, numbness, or tingling in the face or tongue, which can cause garbled speech."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Occur most often at night, or when falling asleep or waking up."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It usually lasts up to two minutes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Seizures typically stop by the time a child is 19 years old."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Some children with BRE have problems with learning, speech, or behavioural issues."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In most cases, children with BRE don't need medication because the seizures aren't a big problem. Most children stop having seizures within 2 to 4 years after they begin."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Febrile seizures (Option C) are the most common type of epilepsy in infants and young children aged 6 months to 5 years, with a peak incidence between 12 and 18 months."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"They are associated with febrile illnesses but have no identifiable cause, such as CNS infection or metabolic issues."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Simple FS: Lasts less than 15 minutes, typically associated with common infections. High risk of developing temporal lobe epilepsy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Complex FS: Involves multiple seizures during a single febrile illness, lasting 15-30 minutes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Febrile Status Epilepticus (FSE): Seizures lasting over 30 minutes, accounting for 25-52% of status epilepticus cases in children."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A higher risk of FSE is noted in children with underlying neurological disorders."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Myoclonic seizures (Option D) are less common than subtle seizures. They are rapid, single, or rhythmic repetitive jerks that involve a finger, limb, or the entire body. They are more common in pre-term infants than term infants. It may resemble the Moro reflex and startling responses. It is associated with severe brain damage."}]},{"n":11,"q":"A 25 y/o woman with a history of catamenial epilepsy reports an increase in seizure frequency just before her menstrual period. She is currently taking carbamazepine and is newly started on OCPs for contraception. What is the most appropriate advice for her regarding her current situation?","o":[{"t":"Continue carbamazepine without changes."},{"t":"Switch to phenytoin for better control."},{"t":"Consider alternative contraception methods due to carbamazepine's effect on oral contraceptives."},{"t":"Stop all antiseizure medications while trying to conceive."}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Consider alternative contraception methods due to carbamazepine's effect on oral contraceptives."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Carbamazepine can significantly reduce the effectiveness of oral contraceptives, so alternative methods (like IUDs) are recommended for women not planning to conceive."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Catamenial Increased seizure frequency around menses due to Epilepsy estrogen/progesterone effects or antiseizure drug level"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"changes. Some women may require increased antiseizure drug dosages during menses. Natural progestins or intramuscular medroxyprogesterone may benefit certain women."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pregnancy ~50% of women experience unchanged seizure"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"frequency; ~30% may increase, and ~20% decrease. Monitoring of serum antiseizure drug levels is essential during pregnancy. Lamotrigine and levetiracetam are recommended as first-line monotherapy for generalized and focal onset seizures. The higher teratogenic risk with multiple antiseizure drugs is noted. Valproic acid is strongly associated with adverse fetal outcomes and is thus contraindicated in pregnancy. Folate supplementation (1–4 mg/d) is recommended due to antifolate effects of anticonvulsants. Enzyme-inducing drugs (phenytoin, carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine, topiramate, phenobarbital, primidone) may cause vitamin K deficiency in newborns. Hence mothers should take oral vitamin K, and infants should receive intramuscular vitamin K at birth."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Contraception Certain antiseizure medications (carbamazepine,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"phenytoin, phenobarbital, topiramate) reduce oral contraceptive efficacy via enzyme induction. Alternative contraception methods (IUDs, long-acting reversible contraceptives) should be considered."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Breast-Feeding Antiseizure medications are excreted in breast milk at"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"varying levels (5% for valproic acid to 300% for levetiracetam). Encouraged to breastfeed unless infant shows signs of drug effects (e.g., lethargy, poor feeding)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Continue carbamazepine without changes (Option A): Carbamazepine reduces oral contraceptive efficacy, requiring adjustments."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Switch to phenytoin for better control (Option B): Phenytoin also interacts with contraceptives and has teratogenic risks."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Stop all antiseizure medications while trying to conceive (Option D): Uncontrolled seizures pose risks, so effective seizure control should be maintained."}]},{"n":12,"q":"A 10 y/o boy presents to the clinic with a history of seizures characterised by stiffness and rhythmic jerking of the entire body that occur multiple times a week. He has tried several medications but continues to experience seizures. Which of the following would be the most appropriate first-line treatment for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Ethosuximide"},{"t":"Lamotrigine"},{"t":"Carbamazepine"},{"t":"Topiramate"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Lamotrigine"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient’s seizures are characterized by stiffness and rhythmic jerking of the entire body, which are hallmark features of generalized tonic-clonic seizures (GTCS). Lamotrigine is a first-line treatment for generalized-onset tonic-clonic seizures, making it the most appropriate choice for this patient."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Type of Seizure First-Line Treatments"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Generalized-Onset Tonic-Clonic Lamotrigine (Option B)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Valproic acid"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Focal Lamotrigine"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Carbamazepine (Option C ruled out) Oxcarbazepine Eslicarbazepine Phenytoin Levetiracetam"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Typical Absence Valproic acid"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Ethosuximide (Option A ruled out) Lamotrigine"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Atypical Absence, Myoclonic, Atonic Valproic acid"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Lamotrigine Topiramate (Option D ruled out)"}]},{"n":13,"q":"Which statement about Mesial Temporal Lobe Epilepsy Syndrome is FALSE?","o":[{"t":"EEG may show unilateral or bilateral anterior temporal spikes."},{"t":"MRI typically reveals an enlarged hippocampus with trilaminar architecture."},{"t":"It is often refractory to anticonvulsants but may respond well to surgical intervention."},{"t":"Auras, postictal disorientation and memory loss can occur."}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) MRI typically reveals an enlarged hippocampus with trilaminar architecture."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"MRI findings in Mesial Temporal Lobe Epilepsy usually show a smaller hippocampus with a loss of the normal trilaminar architecture, reflecting the structural changes associated with the syndrome."},{"k":"img","v":8030},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mesial Temporal lobe Epilepsy Syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"History Often associated with a history of febrile seizures"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Rarely involves generalised seizures A family history of epilepsy may be present Seizures can remit but may reoccur Typically has an early onset"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clinical Observations(Option D Auras are commonly experienced. ruled out) Postictal disorientation."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Behavioural arrest or staring episodes. Memory loss can occur. Presence of complex automatisms. Dysphasia may occur if the focus is in the dominant hemisphere. Unilateral posturing is observed."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Laboratory Studies EEG may show unilateral or bilateral anterior temporal"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"spikes. (Option A ruled out) Interictal PET scans reveal hypometabolism. Ictal SPECT scans demonstrate hyperperfusion. Intracranial amobarbital (Wada) test may indicate material-specific memory deficits."},{"k":"img","v":8030},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"MRI Findings"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"The hippocampus appears smaller with increased signal on T2-weighted sequences. Loss of the trilaminar architecture of the hippocampus. (Option B) ... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pathologic Findings Characterized by a selective loss of specific cell"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"populations within the hippocampus in most cases Dispersion of the granule cell layer and gliosis are noted."},{"k":"img","v":8036},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Refractory to anticonvulsants. Responds well to Surgical intervention. (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"img","v":8042}]},{"n":14,"q":"A 30 y/o man is brought to the neurology clinic for evaluation after a brief period of unconsciousness. He is awake and alert during the examination. An EEG is performed while his eyes are closed. What is the frequency range recorded in the patient’s EEG?","o":[{"t":"8-13 Hz"},{"t":">13 Hz"},{"t":"<4 Hz"},{"t":"4-8 Hz"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) 8-13 Hz"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient’s EEG shows alpha waves , as he is awake with his eyes closed."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Normal Brain Electrical Activity"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Wave Situation Frequen"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"cy"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Alpha Awake with eyes closed 8–13 Hz (Option A)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Beta Most frequently seen. Enhanced in >13 Hz (Option B ruled out) drowsiness and sedative drugs like"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"benzodiazepines."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Theta When sleeping. Drifting to sleep. 4–8 Hz (Option D ruled out) NREM Stage 1 sleep"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Sigma Also called as sleep spindles 12–14"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(associated with K complexes) Hz Deeper sleep NREM Stage 2 sleep"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Delta NREM Stage 3 sleep <4 Hz (Option C ruled out) Deepest sleep"},{"k":"img","v":8045},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"EEG - Brain waves"},{"k":"img","v":8047}]},{"n":15,"q":"A 7 y/o girl presents to the pediatric neurology clinic with a history of multiple seizure types. Her parents report that she has experienced GTCS, atonic seizures where she suddenly drops to the ground, and atypical absence seizures. The child has also shown signs of cognitive impairment. Which of the following statements is TRUE regarding her condition?","o":[{"t":"Characterized by bilateral myoclonic jerks that are most frequent in the morning."},{"t":"The EEG findings show diffuse, symmetric, bilateral 4 to 6 Hz polyspike and wave discharges."},{"t":"Valproate and Topiramate are used in treatment."},{"t":"The condition typically presents in early adolescence."}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Valproate and Topiramate are used in treatment."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The diagnosis is likely Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome, characterized by multiple seizure types, including generalized tonic-clonic, atonic, and atypical absence seizures, along with cognitive impairment in a child. The presence of these varied seizures and cognitive issues supports this diagnosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Valproate and Topiramate are among the first-line treatment options for Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Chara Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome Juvenile Myoclonic Epilepsy (JME/ cteristi JANZ Syndrome)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"cs"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Onset Occurs in children Early adolescence (Option D ruled"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cause Associated with various CNS Unknown cause; polygenic factors"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"dysfunction causes (e.g., de novo suggested mutations, developmental abnormalities, perinatal hypoxia)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Seizur Multiple seizure types, including Primarily bilateral myoclonic jerks; e Type generalized tonic-clonic, atonic, and may also have generalized s atypical absence seizures tonic-clonic and absence seizures"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Myoclo Not a primary feature Most frequent in the morning after nic Sei awakening; can be provoked by zures sleep deprivation. (Option A ruled"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Consci Impaired cognitive function in most Preserved unless myoclonus is ousne cases severe ss"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"EEG F Shows slow (<3 Hz) spike-and-wave Shows diffuse, symmetric, bilateral indings discharges. 4 to 6 Hz polyspike and wave"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"discharges, predominantly in the fronto-central region. (Option B ruled out) Ictal EEG recordings reveal 10-16 Hz polyspike discharges that correlate with myoclonic jerks."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatm Valproate, lamotrigine, and Valproic acid ent topiramate"}]},{"n":16,"q":"A 25 y/o man is brought to the ED after experiencing a generalized tonic-clonic seizure. An EEG is performed after the seizure. Which of the following statements is TRUE regarding the EEG findings associated with his seizure?","o":[{"t":"The interictal EEG is often normal in patients."},{"t":"During the tonic phase, the EEG typically shows a progressive increase in generalized low-voltage fast activity."},{"t":"The postictal EEG usually reveals localized spikes corresponding to the seizure focus."},{"t":"The EEG during the clonic phase shows continuous high-amplitude activity without interruption."}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) During the tonic phase, the EEG typically shows a progressive increase in generalized low-voltage fast activity."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"In the case of GTCS, The EEG during the tonic phase shows a progressive increase in generalized low-voltage fast activity."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Focal Seizure- EEG GTCS- EEG The routine interictal EEG in patients with focal seizures During the tonic phase , the EEG shows a progressive is often normal. (Option A ruled out) increase in generalized low-voltage fast activity. (Option It may show brief discharges, such as epileptiform B) spikes or sharp waves. This is followed by generalized high-amplitude polyspike Focal seizures can arise from areas like the medial discharges. temporal lobe or inferior frontal lobe, which may be In the clonic phase , high-amplitude activity is typically distant from the scalp. interrupted by slow waves, creating a As a result, the EEG recorded during the seizure may spike-and-slow-wave pattern. (Option D ruled out) be nonlocalizing. Generalized seizures tend to terminate synchronously The region of seizure onset can be identified using across widespread brain regions. surgically placed intracranial electrodes. The postictal EEG displays diffuse suppression of all"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"cerebral activity, followed by slowing that gradually recovers as the patient awakens. (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"img","v":8054},{"k":"img","v":8061},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"}]},{"n":17,"q":"A 25 y/o patient faints while standing in a crowded, hot room. Before fainting, he felt dizzy, sweaty, and nauseous. He regains consciousness quickly without any prolonged confusion or disorientation afterwards. What is the most likely diagnosis for this patient's fainting episode?","o":[{"t":"Generalized Tonic-Clonic Seizure (GTCS)"},{"t":"Vasovagal Syncope"},{"t":"Cardiac Arrhythmia"},{"t":"Hypoglycemia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer : B) Vasovagal Syncope"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient’s premonitory symptoms (dizziness, sweating, nausea), environmental triggers (hot, crowded room), brief loss of consciousness, and rapid recovery without postictal confusion are characteristic of vasovagal syncope."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Vasovagal Syncope vs. Generalized Tonic-Clonic Seizures (GTCS) :"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Featu Vasovagal Syncope (Neurally Generalized Tonic-Clonic Seizures res Mediated Syncope) (GTCS)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Caus It results from a reflex that alters GTCS are caused by widespread e autonomic outflow, leading to abnormal electrical activity in the"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"bradycardia, vasodilation, brain, leading to both andreduced cardiac output. This tonic(stiffening) and clonic lowerssystemic blood pressure (jerking)muscle contractions. andcauses cerebral hypoperfusion,le ading to syncope."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Trigg Emotional stress, pain, standing GTCS can occur without clear ers upright for long periods, warm triggers."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"environments, dehydration, blood loss, and sight of blood."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Patho Increased parasympathetic outflow Seizures begin abruptly, often physi (vagal activation) and inhibition of without warning. The tonic phase ology sympathetic outflow result in involves sustained muscle"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"bradycardia and hypotension. contraction, leading to symptoms Cerebral blood flow falls below like the \"ictal cry\" due to vocal cord compensatory limits, causing loss of contraction. The clonic phase consciousness. features rhythmic jerking as"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"muscles relax intermittently."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Symp Premonitory : Dizziness, Premonitory : Some patients have toms lightheadedness, nausea, yawning, vague premonitory symptoms, but"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"sweating, pallor, and palpitations. stereotypical auras are rare. During the event : Transient loss of During the event : Tonic contraction consciousness, uprolling of the followed by clonic jerking. The eyes, dilated pupils, possible patient may experience cyanosis, muscle jerks (myoclonus), and tongue biting, loss of bladder/bowel rarely, urinary or faecal incontinence. control, and increased heart rate EEG pattern : \"Slow-flat-slow\" and blood pressure. pattern with high-amplitude slow Postictal : Period of confusion waves, followed by EEG flattening. lasting minutes to hours, fatigue,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"headache, muscle soreness, and incontinence. Postictal confusion is a prominent feature. EEG pattern : Low-voltage fast activity during the tonic phase, followed by a spike-and-slow-wave pattern during the clonic phase. Postictal suppression of brain activity is observed."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Reco Quick recovery of consciousness GTCS has a prolonged postictal very without prolonged confusion. phase with confusion and fatigue."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Postictal confusion and hallucinations are rare."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Generalized Tonic-Clonic Seizure (GTCS) (Option A) : Unlikely, as GTCS usually involves tonic-clonic movements and prolonged postictal confusion, neither of which occurred."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cardiac Arrhythmia (Option C) : Less likely, as arrhythmias often lack clear premonitory symptoms like dizziness or nausea, and recovery may not be as immediate."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hypoglycemia (Option D) : Unlikely, as hypoglycemia often presents with hunger, sweating, and confusion, but without a clear environmental trigger like standing in a hot room."}]},{"n":18,"q":"A 6-month-old infant presents with brief episodes of sudden flexion of the trunk and limbs, lasting a few seconds. EEG shows hypsarrhythmia, and during the episodes, there's marked background suppression on the EEG. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Absence seizures"},{"t":"Myoclonic seizures"},{"t":"Epileptic spasms"},{"t":"Lennox-Gastaut syndrome"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Epileptic spasms"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: The clinical description of brief, sustained flexion, age group (infants), and EEG showing hypsarrhythmia with background suppression are classic for epileptic spasms."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Epileptic spasms:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Definition : Epileptic spasms are characterized by brief, sustained flexion or extension of mainly proximal muscles, including the trunk. Also known as Salaam convulsions ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Age Group : Predominantly occurs in infants, likely due to differences in neuronal function and connectivity in the immature CNS."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"EEG Features : Shows hypsarrhythmia, marked by diffuse giant slow waves with a chaotic background of irregular , multifocal spikes and sharp waves."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical Spasm EEG : During a spasm, the EEG shows significant background suppression, known as the \"electrodecremental response.\""},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"EMG Characteristics : Reveals a rhomboid pattern, distinguishing it from brief tonic and myoclonic seizures."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Absence seizures (Option A) : Absence seizures are generalized seizures characterized by brief lapses in consciousness without the tonic or flexor posturing, and no hypsarrhythmia pattern on EEG."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Myoclonic seizures (Option B) : Myoclonic seizures are brief, sudden jerks without sustained flexion/extension or characteristic EEG changes (hypsarrhythmia or electrodecremental response)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lennox-Gastaut syndrome (Option D) : Though this syndrome also occurs in children and involves multiple seizure types, it does not typically present with hypsarrhythmia or spasms as seen here."}]},{"n":19,"q":"A 23 y/o patient presents with sudden, brief muscle jerks involving both arms. EEG shows bilaterally synchronous spike-and-slow-wave discharges. The patient has a history of consuming poor-quality beef. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Epileptic spasms"},{"t":"Juvenile Myoclonic Epilepsy"},{"t":"Variant Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (vCJD)"},{"t":"Anoxic brain injury"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Variant Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (vCJD)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The diagnosis is most likely “ Variant Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (vCJD)”, due to the patient’s history of consuming poor-quality beef, which is associated with prion diseases like vCJD, a known cause of myoclonus."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Myoclonic Seizures :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Definition : Sudden, brief muscle contractions that can involve a part of or the entire body."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Physiological Example : Normal myoclonus occurs as a sudden jerk when falling asleep."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pathological Causes : Most commonly seen in metabolic disorders, degenerative CNS diseases, or anoxic brain injury. VCJD -Variant Creutzfeldt Jakob Disease: Patient has a history of consumption of poor-quality beef. It may contain Prion particles, which, on entering the body, multiply in the cytoplasm of neurons. It irritates neurons, leading to their abnormal firing."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"VCJD -Variant Creutzfeldt Jakob Disease: Patient has a history of consumption of poor-quality beef. It may contain Prion particles, which, on entering the body, multiply in the cytoplasm of neurons. It irritates neurons, leading to their abnormal firing."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aetiology : Myoclonic seizures are epileptic events caused by cortical dysfunction, as opposed to subcortical or spinal causes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"EEG Findings : Shows bilaterally synchronous spike-and-slow-wave discharges just before the myoclonic jerk, with muscle artefact."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Association with Other Seizures : Myoclonic seizures often occur alongside other generalized seizures. They are the hallmark feature of juvenile myoclonic epilepsy (JME)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"VCJD -Variant Creutzfeldt Jakob Disease: Patient has a history of consumption of poor-quality beef. It may contain Prion particles, which, on entering the body, multiply in the cytoplasm of neurons. It irritates neurons, leading to their abnormal firing."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Epileptic spasms (Option A) typically occur in infants and present with different EEG findings (hypsarrhythmia) rather than spike-and-slow-wave discharges."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Juvenile Myoclonic Epilepsy (Option B) : Although JME can cause myoclonic seizures and spike-and-slow-wave discharges, the history of beef consumption strongly suggests prion disease."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Anoxic brain injury (Option D) : There's no evidence of hypoxia or brain injury, and the patient’s symptoms are better explained by prion-related vCJD."}]},{"n":20,"q":"A 7 y/o child is brought to the clinic due to frequent episodes of \"daydreaming\" during school, which last for a few seconds and are accompanied by subtle eyelid blinking. The episodes occur several times a day, and the child has no recollection of these events. An EEG shows 3-Hz generalized spike-and-slow-wave discharges. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Typical absence seizures"},{"t":"Atypical absence seizures"},{"t":"Focal seizures with impaired awareness"},{"t":"Generalized tonic-clonic seizures"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Typical Absence Seizures"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: The clinical scenario of brief \"daydreaming\" episodes with subtle motor signs and the presence of 3-Hz generalized spike-and-slow-wave discharges on EEG is characteristic of Typical absence seizures ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Generalized Onset Seizures:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Arise from a point in the brain but quickly engage both cerebral hemispheres."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Classified into types based on distinct clinical features."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Typical Absence Seizures (Option A) Atypical Absence Seizures (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Characteristics: Sudden, brief lapses of consciousness Characteristics: Longer, less abrupt lapses of without postural control loss, lasting seconds. consciousness with more noticeable motor signs. Motor Signs: Subtle, bilateral motor signs such as eyelid EEG Findings: Slower (<2.5 Hz) generalized blinking, chewing, or small clonic hand movements. spike-and-slow-wave discharges, plus other Onset: Common in children (ages 4–10) or early abnormalities. adolescence, occurring hundreds of times a day. Association: Linked to structural brain abnormalities and Clinical Presentation: May appear as \"daydreaming\" and often co-occurs with mental retardation or neurologic poor school performance, often misdiagnosed as dysfunction. attention deficit disorder. Treatment Response: Less responsive to EEG Findings: Generalized 3-Hz spike-and-slow-wave anticonvulsants compared to typical absence seizures. discharges. Provoked By: Hyperventilation may induce seizures and EEG discharges. Treatment: Valproate (drug of choice) Valproate-associated fulminant hepatic failure (younger the age, higher the risk) Ethosuximide is used (especially in <2 years)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Focal Seizures with Impaired Awareness (Option C): Typically involve a motionless stare and automatisms. It lasts for a longer duration of time and does not have the characteristic 3-Hz generalized discharges on EEG."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Generalized Tonic-Clonic Seizures (Option D): Involve a loss of consciousness with stiffening and jerking of the limbs, which differs from the brief, subtle presentation of absence seizures in this case."}]},{"n":21,"q":"A 35 y/o woman experiences sudden unresponsiveness during conversations, followed by repetitive hand movements and lip-smacking. Afterwards, she seems confused and does not remember the event. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Absence Seizure"},{"t":"Panic Attack"},{"t":"Focal Seizure with Impaired Awareness"},{"t":"Focal Seizure with Intact Awareness"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Focal Seizure with Impaired Awareness"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation: The patient's unresponsiveness, automatisms, and post-seizure confusion are characteristics of focal seizure with impaired awareness."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Focal Seizure with Impaired Awareness"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Impaired Awareness : During the seizure, patients lose their ability to respond to external stimuli and are unaware of the event. They may not recall the seizure (ictal phase) afterwards."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aura : Seizures often begin with an aura, where patients experience unusual internal sensations such as fear, déjà vu, depersonalisation, or visual distortions like micropsia (objects appearing smaller) or macropsia (objects appearing larger). These are subjective events not observable by others."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Motionless Stare : The ictal phase is typically marked by a sudden, motionless stare, signalling the onset of impaired awareness."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Automatisms : Patients may perform automatic, involuntary behaviours like chewing, lip-smacking, swallowing, or repetitive hand movements. More complex actions, like displaying emotions or running, may also occur."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Post-Seizure Confusion : After the seizure, patients are often confused, and the recovery time can range from seconds to over an hour."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Postictal Symptoms : Neurological deficits such as anterograde amnesia, aphasia, or visual disturbances may follow the seizure, depending on which cortical regions were involved."},{"k":"img","v":8069},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Image showing abnormal high voltage in right temporal leads. Since the abnormal electrical activity is seen only in some leads, it is a focal seizure."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Absence Seizure (Option A): Typically involves brief episodes of staring without postictal confusion or automatisms, which is more common in children."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Panic Attack (Option B): Presents with intense fear and physical symptoms, but does not include automatisms or loss of awareness."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Focal Seizure with Intact Awareness (Option D): Involves preserved awareness throughout the event, which is not seen in this case."}]},{"n":22,"q":"A 32 y/o woman experiences involuntary movements that begin in her right hand and progressively spread to her right arm and face. She remains fully aware throughout the episode. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Absence seizure"},{"t":"Todd’s paralysis"},{"t":"Epilepsia partialis continua"},{"t":"Jacksonian march"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: D) Jacksonian march"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient has focal involvement without impairment of awareness . Jacksonian March describes a focal motor seizure where abnormal movements start in one part of the body, like the hand, and gradually spread to other nearby areas, such as the arm and face . The seizure progresses in a “marching” pattern, following the motor cortex's representation of the body."},{"k":"img","v":8072},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Absence seizure (Option A): This seizure typically involves a brief loss of awareness, where the person appears to “zone out” and is unresponsive. It is predominantly seen in children. Motor symptoms are usually absent , and the person does not experience the spreading involuntary movements seen in this patient."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Todd’s paralysis (Option B): Todd’s paralysis is a temporary weakness (paresis) affecting the same area involved in a seizure. It usually occurs after the seizure has ended ( postictally ), which is not the case in this patient. It is also a feature of focal motor seizures with intact awareness."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Epilepsia partialis continua (Option C): This refers to prolonged, continuous focal motor seizures, often restricted to one part of the body, lasting hours or even days . While awareness is usually intact, the condition is characterised by ongoing motor activity rather than the brief spreading movements described here."}]},{"n":23,"q":"A 40 y/o man collapses suddenly while walking and experiences a brief period of unconsciousness. His family notes that his body became completely limp before he fell. When he regains consciousness, he is confused and has a headache. What type of seizure does he most likely have?","o":[{"t":"Clonic seizure"},{"t":"Tonic-clonic seizure"},{"t":"Atonic seizure"},{"t":"Myoclonic seizure"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: C) Atonic seizure"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Classification of Generalised Seizures"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Generalised seizures are characterised by abnormal electrical activity that rapidly spreads across both hemispheres of the brain."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tonic-Clonic Seizures (Option B ruled out) These can occur alone or in combination with other"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"seizure types. Involves muscle stiffening (tonic phase), followed by rhythmic jerking (clonic phase)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Absence Seizures Typical: Brief lapses in consciousness, without loss of"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"postural control. Common in children. May be mistaken for daydreaming and attention deficit disorder. Atypical: Longer episodes, slower onset/offset, may involve other motor symptoms. With Special Features: Specific characteristics like eyelid flutter or additional movements."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Myoclonic Seizures (Option D ruled out) Sudden jerking movements of muscles."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Myoclonic-Atonic: Jerks followed by loss of muscle tone. Myoclonic-Tonic: Jerks accompanied by muscle stiffening."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Atonic Seizures (Option C) Sudden loss of muscle tone, leading to falls . These"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"seizures are brief but pose a high risk of injury due to the sudden nature of the fall."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Clonic Seizures (Option A ruled out) Rhythmic jerking without the initial tonic phase."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tonic Seizures Increased muscle tone with impaired awareness."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Usually seen as a part of epilepsy syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Epileptic spasm They typically occur in infants and are marked by"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"sudden, forceful contractions of the muscles, particularly those of the trunk. The spasms often occur inclusters(5-50), especially upon waking, and areassociated with widespread brain abnormalities."}]},{"n":24,"q":"A 45 y/o man experiences sudden visual disturbances, seeing flashing lights and coloured shapes, but remains fully awake during the episode. After a few seconds, the sensations subside. Which type of seizure is most likely?","o":[{"t":"Focal seizure with retained awareness"},{"t":"Focal seizure with impaired awareness"},{"t":"Absence seizure"},{"t":"Generalised tonic-clonic seizure"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: A) Focal seizure with retained awareness"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"As the patient experiences localized visual disturbances, such as flashing lights and coloured shapes, without any loss of awareness, it is a focal seizure with retained awareness. In focal seizures with retained awareness, individuals can respond to their environment and are aware of their experiences during the seizure."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Classification of Focal Seizures"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Focal seizures originate from a specific area in one hemisphere of the brain."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Classification by Awareness"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Focal Seizures with Retained Awareness No loss of awareness during the seizure, symptoms"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"depend on the brain area affected like visual disturbances, motor jerking, and sensory changes. (Option A)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Focal Seizures with Impaired Awareness Awareness is impaired , often starting with a blank stare"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"and involving automatisms like lip-smacking, picking, chewing and other repetitive movements. (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Motor vs Nonmotor Onset (In Focal Seizures with Retained Awareness)"},{"k":"img","v":8072},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Motor Onset"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"... (content truncated)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Nonmotor Onset Sensory, autonomic, or emotional changes without"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"visible movement."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Evolution"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Focal Seizures Evolving into generalised Start focally but spread to both hemispheres, often Seizures leading to generalised tonic-clonic seizures."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Absence seizure (Option C): Absence seizures typically involve brief lapses in consciousness or staring spells, which are not mentioned in this case. The patient retains awareness throughout the event, which is inconsistent with an absence seizure."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Generalised tonic-clonic seizure (Option D): This seizure involves tonic (muscle stiffening) and clonic (jerking) phases. These often result in postictal confusion and convulsion, unlike the patient’s features of visual disturbances."}]},{"n":25,"q":"A 28 y/o woman is witnessed having a seizure. After a brief period of muscle stiffening, she begins to have jerking movements. The episode lasts about one minute, and she becomes unresponsive with flaccid muscles afterwards. She later regains consciousness but is confused. Which phase of the seizure involves alternating muscle contraction and relaxation?","o":[{"t":"Tonic phase"},{"t":"Clonic phase"},{"t":"Post-ictal period"},{"t":"Aura"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Answer: B) Clonic phase"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The clinical features associated with Generalised Tonic-Clonic Seizure are described. It is a type of seizure that starts abruptly , often without a clear warning."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Generalised Tonic-Clonic Seizure - Features"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Tonic phase (Option A ruled out) Clonic phase (Option B) Post-ictal period (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Muscle contraction throughout Alternating muscle contraction and Unresponsiveness and muscle the body. relaxation. flaccidity. Ictal cry: Loud sound from Relaxation periods gradually Excessive salivation and laboured laryngeal contraction. increase until the seizure ends breathing. Cyanosis: Due to respiratory (usually lasts <1 minute). Incontinence may occur. compromise. Gradual return of consciousness, Tongue biting and sympathetic with confusion, headache, fatigue, activity (increased heart rate, and muscle pain. blood pressure). Lasts about 10-20 seconds"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Aura (Option D): Auras are sensations or symptoms that occur before focal seizures, not during generalised tonic-clonic seizures. There may be a presence of premonitory symptoms in GTCS."}]}]);
QB.imgs({"7939":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8069":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8072":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","7955":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","7960":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","7967":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","7973":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","7979":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","7986":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","7991":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","7997":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8002":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8008":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8021":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8030":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","7904":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8036":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","7909":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","7914":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8042":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8045":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8047":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","7922":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8054":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","7927":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","7932":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","8061":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"});